Latencies are means across the query grid; pushdown and the row-wise baseline
are shown separately because their ratio mixes both.

┌─────────────┬────────┬────────────┬─────────────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────┬───────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
│ Granularity │ Chunks │ Rows/chunk │ Pruning rate (mean) │ Pushdown (mean) │ Row-wise (mean) │ Vectorized (mean) │ Row-wise / pushdown (geomean) │
├─────────────┼────────┼────────────┼─────────────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ Weekly      │ 1,010  │ 5.0        │ 98.87%              │ 7.136ms         │ 298.751ms       │ 253.783ms         │ 44.19x                        │
│ Monthly     │ 240    │ 20.9       │ 98.80%              │ 3.738ms         │ 112.804ms       │ 67.765ms          │ 30.55x                        │
│ Quarterly   │ 80     │ 62.6       │ 97.92%              │ 3.293ms         │ 74.141ms        │ 29.092ms          │ 22.56x                        │
└─────────────┴────────┴────────────┴─────────────────────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────┴───────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

Measured findings (data-driven; this run only)
  Lowest mean pushdown latency : Quarterly
  Lowest mean row-wise latency : Quarterly
  Highest mean pruning rate    : Weekly
  Fewest managed chunks        : Quarterly
  Highest speedup ratio        : Weekly
  Configured engine default    : Monthly
  Default vs measured best     : the configured default (Monthly) did NOT have the lowest mean pushdown latency; Quarterly did (3.293ms vs 3.738ms)

  Reading the speedup column
    From the coarsest (Quarterly, 80 chunks) to the finest (Weekly, 1,010
    chunks) granularity, mean row-wise baseline latency goes from 74.141ms to
    298.751ms and mean pushdown latency from 3.293ms to 7.136ms. The speedup
    ratio mixes both movements: a baseline that slows with chunk count
    inflates it without any pushdown improvement, so the ratio alone is not
    evidence that a granularity is better for pushdown.

  Limitation
    Synthetic data, 10 symbols x 501 rows, 9 queries (one symbol set, three
    date windows, a median-close price band) on one machine. This benchmark
    cannot justify a general default: the best granularity depends on query
    shape, dataset distribution and write workload, and differences between
    granularities may be within run-to-run noise. No universal optimum is
    claimed.

